{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_oywjhg4bdvi3phq3vjxi5rkx7dxaywbru6b7etpkzk7hpbbcbd7q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01BH9bWzAUqkxzdibr2j9krz","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-023p2n/translated_rust/ERRORS.md","content":"# ERRORS.md — error / rejection surface table (Phase A, gate for Phase C)\n\nMechanically derived from `c_src/src/main.c`. The grep for rejection constructs\nis *empty on purpose*:\n\n```\n$ grep -n -E 'RETURN_ERROR|assert|errno|NULL|return -1|return 0;|exit\\(' c_src/src/main.c\n66:    return 0;          # main's success return, not an error path\n```\n\nThere is **no** `assert`, **no** null check, **no** range check, **no** error\nenum and **no** error return in the C. Consequently the entire error surface is\nimplicit and lives in exactly three places:\n\n1. `scanf(\"%d\", &x)` on line 63 — its return value is **discarded**, so every\n   rejection it performs (input failure = EOF, matching failure, out-of-range\n   conversion) manifests only as \"`x` keeps its initialiser `0`\" or as a\n   truncated value.\n2. Signed-integer overflow in `add_bedrooms` (`house->bedrooms += extra`,\n   line 40) and `add_floor` (`house->floors++`, line 36) — UB in the standard,\n   two's-complement wrap in the compiled C.\n3. `printf` on line 48 — its return value is discarded, so write failures are\n   silently ignored (or kill the process via `SIGPIPE`).\n\n`main` is declared `int main()` (no parameters), so `argc`/`argv` are never\ninspected and can never be rejected. `run` takes a plain `int`, so there is no\nenum parameter — the entire `int` range is accepted; there is no invalid value\nto reject (rows 20–23 pin the extremes down anyway).\n\n| #  | function | trigger (the exact invalid input/condition) | expected C result |\n|----|----------|----------------------------------------------|-------------------|\n| 1  | `main`/`scanf` | stdin empty (immediate EOF) | input failure, `scanf` → `EOF`, `x` stays `0`; 8 lines printed for `x=0`; exit `0` |\n| 2  | `main`/`scanf` | stdin is whitespace only (`\" \\t\\n\\v\\f\\r\"`), then EOF | whitespace skipped, then input failure; `x` stays `0`; exit `0` |\n| 3  | `main`/`scanf` | first non-whitespace byte is a letter (`\"abc\"`) | matching failure, `scanf` → `0`, nothing stored, `x` stays `0` |\n| 4  | `main`/`scanf` | lone `\"+\"` then EOF | matching failure/EOF, nothing stored, `x` stays `0` |\n| 5  | `main`/`scanf` | lone `\"-\"` then EOF | matching failure/EOF, nothing stored, `x` stays `0` |\n| 6  | `main`/`scanf` | sign followed by non-digit (`\"-x\"`, `\"+ 5\"`, `\"--5\"`) | matching failure, nothing stored, `x` stays `0` |\n| 7  | `main`/`scanf` | `\".\"` / `\",\"` / `\"*\"` (punctuation first) | matching failure, `x` stays `0` |\n| 8  | `main`/`scanf` | NUL byte first (`\"\\0 5\"`) | `'\\0'` is neither space, sign nor digit → matching failure, `x` stays `0` |\n| 9  | `main`/`scanf` | non-ASCII / invalid-UTF-8 byte first (`0xFF`, `0x80`, `\"é\"`) | matching failure, `x` stays `0` (byte-oriented, never a UTF-8 error) |\n| 10 | `main`/`scanf` | hex-looking input `\"0x10\"` | `%d` is base 10: consumes `\"0\"`, rejects `\"x10\"` → `x = 0` |\n| 11 | `main`/`scanf` | digits then garbage `\"5abc\"` | conversion succeeds (`x = 5`), the garbage is never rejected/read |\n| 12 | `main`/`scanf` | two numbers `\"1 2\"` | only the first conversion happens: `x = 1`, `\"2\"` ignored |\n| 13 | `main`/`scanf` | float syntax `\"2.5\"` | `%d` stops at `'.'` → `x = 2` |\n| 14 | `main`/`scanf` | value above `INT_MAX` but inside `long` (`\"2147483648\"`) | glibc converts to `long` then stores `(int)` truncation → `x = -2147483648` |\n| 15 | `main`/`scanf` | `\"4294967296\"` (2^32) | `(int)4294967296 == 0` → `x = 0` |\n| 16 | `main`/`scanf` | exactly `LONG_MAX` `\"9223372036854775807\"` | no saturation; `(int)LONG_MAX == -1` → `x = -1` |\n| 17 | `main`/`scanf` | above `LONG_MAX` (`\"9223372036854775808\"`, `\"99999999999999999999\"`) | strtol saturates to `LONG_MAX` (`ERANGE`, ignored) → stored `(int)` = `-1` |\n| 18 | `main`/`scanf` | exactly `LONG_MIN` `\"-9223372036854775808\"` | `(int)LONG_MIN == 0` → `x = 0` |\n| 19 | `main`/`scanf` | below `LONG_MIN` (`\"-99999999999999999999\"`) | saturates to `LONG_MIN` → stored `(int)` = `0` |\n| 20 | `main`/`scanf` | overflow guard boundary: 19+ digits that are still small (`\"0000000000000000005\"`) | leading zeros do not overflow → `x = 5` |\n| 21 | `main`/`scanf` | `x = INT_MAX` → `add_bedrooms(5 + INT_MAX)` | signed overflow wraps: bedrooms `-2147483644`, second `run` → `3` |\n| 22 | `main`/`scanf` | `x = INT_MIN` (`\"-2147483648\"`) | wraps: bedrooms `-2147483643`, second `run` → `5` |\n| 23 | `run` (FFI) | `extra_bedrooms = INT_MAX` / `INT_MIN` applied repeatedly to the accumulating global | every add wraps modulo 2^32; identical sequence in both libraries |\n| 24 | `run` (FFI) | `floors++` overflow (`INT_MAX` floors) | would need 2^31 calls to reach; unreachable in practice — Rust uses `wrapping_add` so it wraps like the C instead of panicking |\n| 25 | `main`/`scanf` | stdin closed (fd 0 not open → `read` fails `EBADF`) | read error = input failure, `x` stays `0`, still prints 8 lines, exit `0` |\n| 26 | `main`/`scanf` | stdin is a directory (`read` fails `EISDIR`) | read error = input failure, `x` stays `0`, exit `0` |\n| 27 | `main`/`printf` | stdout closed (fd 1 not open) | `printf`/flush failure ignored, no output, exit status `0` |\n| 28 | `main`/`printf` | stdout is a pipe whose reader has exited | `SIGPIPE` at default disposition → process killed by signal 13 (no exit code) |\n| 29 | `main`/`scanf` | token straddling the stdio buffer boundary (>4096 bytes of leading whitespace, or a long digit run) | buffer refill is invisible: same conversion result |\n| 30 | `main` | extra `argv` entries (`driver foo bar`) | `int main()` ignores them; identical output, exit `